{
  "dom_id": "step-q111793",
  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "Consider a linear transformation $T$ that maps ${R}^{2}$ onto ${R}^{2}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\u27e81,1\u27e9\\in \\text{Im}(T)$\n- $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}$\n- $\\text{nullity}(T)=0$, where $T$ is the standard matrix of $T$",
  "worked_solution": "Recall that a linear transformation $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto  ${R}^{2}$ if and only if \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)=\\text{rank}(T)=2\n$$\n\nand, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{nullity}(T) & =2-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =2-2 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, The condition $\\text{rank}(T)=2$ in ${R}^{2}$ is equivalent to \n\n$$\n\\text{Im}(T)={R}^{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, $T$ is surjective and so  $\u27e81,1\u27e9\\in \\text{Im}(T).$\n\n- Statement II is false. The condition $\\text{nullity}(T)=0$ in ${R}^{2}$ is equivalent to \n\n$$\n\\text{Ker}(T)={0}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, we have that \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(T)=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"",
